<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Croatian Parliament</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hrvatski sabor, Sabor, Croatian Sabor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Croatian Parliament, or Sabor, is the legislature of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and one of the oldest representative assemblies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, with records of assemblies of the Croatian nobility from the 13th century. Under Hungarian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule it defended Croatian autonomy, and in October 1918 it declared the break with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. It became the parliament of independent Croatia in 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/croatian-parliament.jpg" alt="Croatian Parliament" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ZericaRibica · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hrvatski_sabor_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The oldest Sabor with surviving records met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> on 19 April 1273, as the general diet of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>, and its decisions had legislative power. The earliest recorded Sabor of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> dates to 1350 at Podgrađe near Benkovac. In 1527 the session at Cetin freely chose Ferdinand I of the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> as ruler, after centuries of personal union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. The Sabor then became a regular gathering of the nobility, and by 1558 its title had stabilised as the Parliament of the Kingdom of Croatia and Slavonia. Since 1681 it has been formally the Diet of the Kingdom of Croatia, Dalmatia and Slavonia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1712 the Sabor again invoked its right to choose the ruler, supporting what became the Pragmatic Sanction of 1713 and electing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> as monarch. During the Croatian-Ottoman Wars sessions became so impractical that in 1685 it had the ban appoint a six-member committee, which began work in 1689 and kept its mandate through the 18th and into the 19th century. Since the mid-1800s the Sabor has met regularly and its members have been regularly elected.</p><p>On 29 October 1918 the Sabor decided on independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> and the formation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a>, which later joined the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>. It did not meet between 1918 and 1945, apart from an unelected Sabor convened in 1942. It reconvened in 1943 as the assembly of the State Anti-fascist Council for the National Liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and evolved through several constitutions after the elections of November 1945. After the first multi-party elections and the constitution of 1990 it was bicameral, with a Chamber of Representatives and a Chamber of Counties, until constitutional amendments in 2001 made it unicameral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Under the constitution the Sabor represents the people and holds legislative power. It has at least 100 and no more than 160 members, elected for four years by direct, universal and equal suffrage and secret ballot, with seats reserved for national minorities and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> abroad. It meets in two regular sessions a year and elects a Speaker. The Sabor has convened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> since the 13th century, but had no special building until the 18th, meeting before then in private houses, royal estates in Gradec and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s residence. It first met in a new building on 6 May 1737. The present building, designed by Lav Kalda and Karlo Susan, was completed in 1911.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Croatian Parliament” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A <b>Croatian Sabor</b> was planned but never elected.</blockquote><a href="/state/banovina-of-croatia">Banovina of Croatia</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom kept its own institutions: a ban governed for the king, and the <b>Sabor</b> (diet) of nobles met separately.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-1102-1526">Kingdom of Croatia (1102-1526)</a></li><li><blockquote>Croatia was governed by a ban and its own <b>Sabor</b>, within the Lands of the Hungarian Crown, while much of its territory was detached into the Military Frontier under Vienna.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-habsburg">Kingdom of Croatia (Habsburg)</a></li><li><blockquote>Croatia kept its own <b>Sabor</b> and control of internal affairs, justice and education, while finance and defence were common with Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-slavonia">Kingdom of Croatia-Slavonia</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1745 the civil counties were placed under the Croatian ban and <b>Sabor</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-slavonia">Kingdom of Slavonia</a></li><li><blockquote>On 29 October the <b>Croatian Sabor</b> broke ties with Hungary and Austria, and the new state was proclaimed, covering Slovenia, Croatia-Slavonia, Dalmatia, Bosnia and Herzegovina and Vojvodina, with the Slovene politician Anton Korošec as president.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a></li></ul></section>
